WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.867 --> 00:00:02.869
- I knew that at some point

00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:05.805
he had to admit that
he was going to use it.

00:00:05.805 --> 00:00:07.674
But I said, "Why not now?"

00:00:07.674 --> 00:00:09.476 align:start size:81% position:19%
And he said, "It's
not ready yet.

00:00:09.476 --> 00:00:11.177
This isn't the time yet."

00:00:11.177 --> 00:00:14.381
So I think what he
is preparing for

00:00:14.381 --> 00:00:16.816 align:start size:84% position:16%
is something really
big on election night.

00:00:16.816 --> 00:00:19.819
(soft serene music)

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:25.425 line:0%
- I'm Dave Rubin and this
is "The Rubin Report".

00:00:25.425 --> 00:00:26.726 line:0%
Quick reminder everybody

00:00:26.726 --> 00:00:28.895 line:0%
to subscribe to our
YouTube channel,

00:00:28.895 --> 00:00:31.831 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
so you actually get
notified of our new videos.

00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:35.368 align:start position:0%
And joining me today is a
former New York State prosecutor

00:00:35.368 --> 00:00:39.439 align:start position:0%
and judge: host of "Justice
with Judge Jeanine" on Fox News

00:00:39.439 --> 00:00:41.307
and author of the new book

00:00:41.307 --> 00:00:45.278
"Don't Lie to Me: And Stop
Trying to Steal Our Freedom".

00:00:45.278 --> 00:00:48.481 align:start size:97% position:3%
Judge Jeanine Pirro, welcome
to "The Rubin Report".

00:00:48.481 --> 00:00:50.350 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well it's a pleasure
to be with you

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:51.918
and on the other end actually.

00:00:51.918 --> 00:00:53.887
And I really appreciate
you plugging my book

00:00:53.887 --> 00:00:55.588 line:0%
right off the bat.

00:00:55.588 --> 00:00:58.491 line:0%
In fact, I have it
right here behind me

00:00:58.491 --> 00:01:00.493 line:0%
right under "Don't
Burn This Book".

00:01:00.493 --> 00:01:03.063
So it as you could see you
know, it's a 50/50 here.

00:01:03.063 --> 00:01:05.598
People will decide
your book or my book.

00:01:05.598 --> 00:01:07.700
Probably your book.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:08.902
- No, no, no, no, no.

00:01:08.902 --> 00:01:10.503
This is gonna be
all about your book

00:01:10.503 --> 00:01:14.074
and I have to tell people
a couple insider things

00:01:14.074 --> 00:01:15.742
before we fully start here.

00:01:15.742 --> 00:01:18.078
Because although we have
not met in real life yet

00:01:18.078 --> 00:01:20.213 align:start size:84% position:16%
because of this
COVID ridiculousness,

00:01:20.213 --> 00:01:21.681
I've been doin'
your show a bunch

00:01:21.681 --> 00:01:24.017
and I feel like we
have this kinship

00:01:24.017 --> 00:01:25.351
even though we haven't actually

00:01:25.351 --> 00:01:26.820
broken bread yet or anything.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:29.222 align:start size:94% position:6%
And one of the things
that strikes me about you

00:01:29.222 --> 00:01:31.558
is I do a lotta the Fox shows

00:01:31.558 --> 00:01:35.995
and on no other show does the
host call the guest before,

00:01:35.995 --> 00:01:38.798 align:start size:88% position:13%
but you call me when
I'm usually on Saturdays

00:01:38.798 --> 00:01:40.233
when you're live.

00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:42.535 align:start size:97% position:3%
You call me in the afternoon,
you ask how I'm doin'

00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:44.270
and then you always say,
"And what's on your mind?

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:45.105
What are you thinkin' about?

00:01:45.105 --> 00:01:46.406
What's goin' on?"

00:01:46.406 --> 00:01:48.842 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that strikes me
as just very different

00:01:48.842 --> 00:01:51.377 align:start size:81% position:19%
than how all of
cable news is done

00:01:51.377 --> 00:01:53.413
that you're just
really interested

00:01:53.413 --> 00:01:57.016 align:start size:94% position:6%
in talking about what
people really care about.

00:01:57.016 --> 00:01:58.685 align:start size:84% position:16%
Is that fair to
say, Judge Jeanine?

00:01:58.685 --> 00:02:00.520
How is that for
a first question?

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:03.723
- Well first of all,
it was very informative

00:02:03.723 --> 00:02:05.258
because I have no idea

00:02:05.258 --> 00:02:07.927
how the other people
do their shows on Fox.

00:02:07.927 --> 00:02:09.362
But what I can tell you is

00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:12.632
maybe it's my background
that speaks to who I am.

00:02:12.632 --> 00:02:14.901
I am a little different
from those people on Fox.

00:02:14.901 --> 00:02:17.570 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean I've been a
prosecutor, a judge and a DA.

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:20.140 align:start size:84% position:16%
For 32 years, I ran
for office five times.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:21.941 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean I have a
different background

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:23.510
and a different skillset.

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:24.777
And to this day,

00:02:24.777 --> 00:02:26.746
I'll say I need a good
witness for this one.

00:02:26.746 --> 00:02:30.183 align:start position:0%
And you know, it's not a
witness it's against obviously.

00:02:30.183 --> 00:02:33.820
But I really think
it's important that
people come on a show

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:36.523
and talk about what's
important to them.

00:02:36.523 --> 00:02:39.025 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
And as long as we
have an intersection

00:02:39.025 --> 00:02:43.463 line:0%
where it's important to me and
I believe then to the public

00:02:43.463 --> 00:02:45.331 line:0%
that that's your
passion, you know?

00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:47.233 align:start size:81% position:19%
That's what you're
interested in,

00:02:47.233 --> 00:02:50.003
then you literally
jump off the screen.

00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:52.305 align:start size:84% position:16%
Because to me,
passion is everything.

00:02:52.305 --> 00:02:56.442
And I've long believed
and I tried jury trials.

00:02:56.442 --> 00:03:00.914
I was a litigator for many
years and it's what moves me.

00:03:00.914 --> 00:03:02.382
It's what moves people.

00:03:02.382 --> 00:03:04.884
So I didn't know any of that

00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:06.586 align:start size:84% position:16%
or that other people
didn't do that,

00:03:06.586 --> 00:03:08.054
but I always like to get a sense

00:03:08.054 --> 00:03:10.557 align:start size:97% position:3%
of what you wanna talk about
and where your head is.

00:03:10.557 --> 00:03:14.093 align:start size:97% position:3%
I don't rely on other
people to screen my guests.

00:03:14.093 --> 00:03:15.562
That's not what I do.

00:03:16.596 --> 00:03:18.097
- Yeah.

00:03:18.097 --> 00:03:20.800
Do you like doin' the TV stuff
more than bein' a prosecutor

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.469
or bein' a judge or do you
just think it's exercising

00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:26.773
like a different skillset
or somethin' like that?

00:03:26.773 --> 00:03:28.942
- Well you gave me a
great outright there,

00:03:28.942 --> 00:03:31.344
exercising a different skillset.

00:03:31.344 --> 00:03:33.079
But the truth is the
best job in the world

00:03:33.079 --> 00:03:34.948
was being a prosecutor, Dave.

00:03:34.948 --> 00:03:37.984
I mean without a doubt
and I'll tell you why.

00:03:37.984 --> 00:03:40.320
I don't mean a prosecutor
in the sense that you know,

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.722
you're out there and you
know, lockin' people up.

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:45.725
No, being a prosecutor to me

00:03:45.725 --> 00:03:48.528 align:start size:84% position:16%
was about making
victims whole again.

00:03:48.528 --> 00:03:50.830 align:start size:84% position:16%
It leads to the
extent that you can.

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:53.633
It might even be just
the victim's families.

00:03:53.633 --> 00:03:57.604
But you know, when I first
started prosecuting crimes,

00:03:57.604 --> 00:04:00.840
I became an assistant DA in 1975

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:02.308
which you know, my
kids will tell you

00:04:02.308 --> 00:04:04.377
that's when they wrote
the laws on stone tablets

00:04:04.377 --> 00:04:05.678
and stopped counting my age.

00:04:05.678 --> 00:04:08.881
I see your eyes. (vocalizes)

00:04:08.881 --> 00:04:12.819 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it was like the
victims were nothing more

00:04:12.819 --> 00:04:15.021
than an element of the case,

00:04:15.021 --> 00:04:18.658
the people of the State of
New York against John Smith,

00:04:18.658 --> 00:04:20.193
all right?

00:04:20.193 --> 00:04:22.695
But to me, the victim was
the reason we were there.

00:04:22.695 --> 00:04:26.065
The victim was the person who
never chose to be a victim,

00:04:26.065 --> 00:04:29.502
who never chose to be the
system in the first place.

00:04:29.502 --> 00:04:31.971
And so my energies were trying

00:04:31.971 --> 00:04:33.473
to make that victim whole again,

00:04:33.473 --> 00:04:36.209
whether the victim was a five
year old who had been raped

00:04:36.209 --> 00:04:37.777
and didn't know the language

00:04:37.777 --> 00:04:39.345
you know, just said "Peachy pie.

00:04:39.345 --> 00:04:40.780
My honey bun."

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:43.850 align:start size:97% position:3%
And teaching them with
anatomically-correct dolls,

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:46.085 align:start size:88% position:13%
how they could
communicate with a jury

00:04:46.085 --> 00:04:50.623
or a senior citizen who ended
up losing you know, her house

00:04:50.623 --> 00:04:53.326 align:start size:88% position:13%
because she was a victim
of economic crimes.

00:04:53.326 --> 00:04:58.331
So that to me was my passion
and I did it for many decades.

00:04:59.265 --> 00:05:00.366
I went to the bench.

00:05:00.366 --> 00:05:02.869
Because to be a judge you know,

00:05:02.869 --> 00:05:05.905 align:start position:0%
it's supposed to be the
citadel or the practice of law.

00:05:05.905 --> 00:05:07.607
Hogwash, it's not.

00:05:07.607 --> 00:05:09.108
I felt like a referee.

00:05:09.108 --> 00:05:13.179
I felt that when I was a
judge sitting on the bench

00:05:13.179 --> 00:05:15.648 align:start size:84% position:16%
you know, I would
sustain an objection,

00:05:15.648 --> 00:05:16.983
overrule an objection.

00:05:16.983 --> 00:05:19.152
They'd come up to
me and they'd argue

00:05:19.152 --> 00:05:21.487
and I wanted to be in the fight.

00:05:21.487 --> 00:05:23.990 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, it's not
really my personality.

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:27.794
So I had a tenure term
and after three years,

00:05:27.794 --> 00:05:30.730
I said I've had enough of
this and I ran for judge.

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:32.765
I was the first woman
judge in the history,

00:05:32.765 --> 00:05:35.535
a county judge in the
history of the county.

00:05:35.535 --> 00:05:38.137
And I said, "I can't
take this anymore.

00:05:38.137 --> 00:05:40.006
This is too slow for me."

00:05:40.006 --> 00:05:41.007
And I remember that

00:05:42.608 --> 00:05:44.777
I spoke to the chief
administrative judge.

00:05:44.777 --> 00:05:46.279
And I said, "You know, judge?

00:05:48.014 --> 00:05:51.851 align:start size:88% position:13%
While I'm taking a plea
with one defendant,

00:05:51.851 --> 00:05:54.020
if you could have
the court officers

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:56.589 align:start size:88% position:13%
bring another defendant
outta the bullpen,

00:05:56.589 --> 00:05:58.191
his lawyer can talk to him.

00:05:58.191 --> 00:06:00.026
And then as soon as
this plea is over,

00:06:00.026 --> 00:06:02.962 align:start size:97% position:3%
he's ready to plea or
whatever the motion could be."

00:06:02.962 --> 00:06:05.098
And I thought I was
so sharp, right?

00:06:05.098 --> 00:06:06.265
And I'd move things along.

00:06:06.265 --> 00:06:08.401 align:start size:88% position:13%
I said, "I could handle
twice the cases."

00:06:08.401 --> 00:06:11.003
And his response was, "Are
you tryin' to make the men

00:06:11.003 --> 00:06:12.405
look bad, Jeanine?"

00:06:12.405 --> 00:06:13.639
(Dave chuckling)

00:06:13.639 --> 00:06:16.242 align:start size:84% position:16%
And you know, I didn't
take it personally.

00:06:16.242 --> 00:06:17.810
But I thought to myself

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:21.381
whether this is sexist
or not, doesn't matter.

00:06:21.381 --> 00:06:23.916 align:start size:84% position:16%
This is not the
speed that I work at.

00:06:23.916 --> 00:06:27.019 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I stepped off the bench and
I ran for district attorney

00:06:27.019 --> 00:06:28.521
because I was a fighter.

00:06:28.521 --> 00:06:31.391
I started the first domestic
violence unit in the country.

00:06:31.391 --> 00:06:33.626
You know, first hate crimes unit

00:06:33.626 --> 00:06:37.029
in the history of my
county, sex crimes unit

00:06:37.029 --> 00:06:40.366
and worked with you know,
John Cardinal O'Connor

00:06:40.366 --> 00:06:43.302
in trying to make sure
that everyone was covered

00:06:43.302 --> 00:06:44.737
under hate crimes.

00:06:44.737 --> 00:06:48.941 align:start size:97% position:3%
It wasn't just you know,
gender and ethnicity and color

00:06:48.941 --> 00:06:51.344
but sexual orientation as well.

00:06:51.344 --> 00:06:54.380
And as president of
the DA's Association,

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:57.750
John Cardinal O'Connor who
was beloved in New York,

00:06:57.750 --> 00:06:59.585
went in and supported that

00:06:59.585 --> 00:07:01.754
and it became part
of New York law.

00:07:01.754 --> 00:07:04.524
So where I saw problems,

00:07:04.524 --> 00:07:07.660
I was able to move
the legislature.

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:09.662 align:start size:84% position:16%
If I had enough
support believe me,

00:07:09.662 --> 00:07:11.831 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't think
singularly I could.

00:07:11.831 --> 00:07:14.967
But I could do something to
try to make a difference.

00:07:14.967 --> 00:07:16.969
Where I saw victimization,

00:07:16.969 --> 00:07:19.005 align:start size:84% position:16%
I would go out there
and publicly say

00:07:19.005 --> 00:07:22.775 align:start size:97% position:3%
with the advent of the
internet, your kids cannot use

00:07:22.775 --> 00:07:26.112
the PC as a babysitter.

00:07:26.112 --> 00:07:29.282 align:start position:0%
And I started the first internet
pedophile sting operation

00:07:29.282 --> 00:07:33.119
in the nation and we arrested
doctors, lawyers, priests,

00:07:33.119 --> 00:07:37.323 align:start position:0%
coaches, school superintendents,
CEOs, you name it.

00:07:37.323 --> 00:07:40.393 align:start size:81% position:19%
And it was very
satisfying for me.

00:07:40.393 --> 00:07:42.495
But I gave that job update

00:07:42.495 --> 00:07:46.399
because I felt that
I had done it enough

00:07:46.399 --> 00:07:51.370
and it was kind of like my
own self-imposed term limits.

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:54.640
But without a doubt, it was
the best job in the world.

00:07:55.842 --> 00:07:58.144
- So what brought
you to cable news?

00:07:58.144 --> 00:08:00.880 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean as if you hadn't had
enough fights in the courtroom

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:03.749 align:start position:0%
and then runnin' for prosecutor
you know, all of this.

00:08:03.749 --> 00:08:05.985
DA and all that, it's like what?

00:08:05.985 --> 00:08:09.188
I mean there's no fight
like a cable news fight.

00:08:09.188 --> 00:08:13.159
- Well I must tell you
that for whatever reason

00:08:13.159 --> 00:08:17.864
I got involved in television
and it was the OJ Simpson case.

00:08:17.864 --> 00:08:22.768
I believe it was 1994
and that Bronco chase.

00:08:22.768 --> 00:08:25.638
And I remember I had
just become the DA

00:08:25.638 --> 00:08:29.542
and I had you know, all 150
assistant DAs at my home.

00:08:29.542 --> 00:08:31.110
And one of the guys came out,

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:32.578
I think it was one
of the investigators.

00:08:32.578 --> 00:08:33.913 align:start size:94% position:6%
He said, "Hey boss, you're
not gonna believe it.

00:08:33.913 --> 00:08:34.747
OJ Simpson's in a Bronco.

00:08:34.747 --> 00:08:36.415
They're chasin' him."

00:08:36.415 --> 00:08:37.817 align:start size:84% position:16%
I said, "What are
they chasing him for?

00:08:37.817 --> 00:08:38.718
Is he speeding?"

00:08:38.718 --> 00:08:40.219
Nobody knew.

00:08:40.219 --> 00:08:44.490
But that was really the advent
of you know, my introduction

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:48.294
into the importance of
the media in identifying

00:08:48.294 --> 00:08:51.731
and trying to you know,
change certain issues

00:08:51.731 --> 00:08:52.965
in our society.

00:08:52.965 --> 00:08:54.934
So for battered women you know,

00:08:54.934 --> 00:08:57.069
I started the first
unit in the nation

00:08:57.069 --> 00:08:59.505
under the Department of Justice.

00:08:59.505 --> 00:09:02.942 align:start size:88% position:13%
And people kept
thinking battered women.

00:09:02.942 --> 00:09:05.778 align:start size:84% position:16%
Sex is better for them
after their beaten

00:09:05.778 --> 00:09:09.815 align:start position:0%
or they deserved it or you
know, "She's a pain in the ass."

00:09:09.815 --> 00:09:12.385
And you know, as a
woman assistant DA,

00:09:12.385 --> 00:09:17.156 align:start position:0%
I had to deal with 43 male
police chiefs in my jurisdiction

00:09:17.156 --> 00:09:19.759
and deal with that kind of...

00:09:19.759 --> 00:09:21.260
There was a discrimination.

00:09:21.260 --> 00:09:24.397
The thinking was marriage is
forever, be a better wife.

00:09:24.397 --> 00:09:25.898 align:start size:84% position:16%
If he's beating you,
be a better wife.

00:09:25.898 --> 00:09:27.033
Go pray.

00:09:27.033 --> 00:09:30.269
And so we were part of a change.

00:09:30.269 --> 00:09:33.039
It made a tremendous difference.

00:09:33.039 --> 00:09:35.207 align:start size:88% position:13%
But I saw the
importance in the media.

00:09:35.207 --> 00:09:38.678
And as a prosecutor, I
used the media to help me

00:09:38.678 --> 00:09:43.449 align:start size:97% position:3%
identify issues and get more
victims to come forward.

00:09:43.449 --> 00:09:45.117
- So just hearing your
history a little bit

00:09:45.117 --> 00:09:47.053
and some of this stuff
I actually didn't know,

00:09:47.053 --> 00:09:49.455 align:start size:84% position:16%
does it drive you
crazy when people say,

00:09:49.455 --> 00:09:51.757
"Oh, conservatives
are anti-women,"

00:09:51.757 --> 00:09:53.793
or "Conservatives are anti-gay,"

00:09:53.793 --> 00:09:55.428
or "Conservatives are racist"?

00:09:55.428 --> 00:09:56.929
Or the rest of it.

00:09:56.929 --> 00:09:58.197
We've talked a little bit
about that on your show.

00:09:58.197 --> 00:09:59.432
But when you hear
some of the things

00:09:59.432 --> 00:10:01.000
that you've been involved in,

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:05.104 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's kinda crazy that people
get these labels on 'em.

00:10:06.539 --> 00:10:10.643 align:start size:97% position:3%
- And you know, the movie that
they made about Roger Ailes

00:10:10.643 --> 00:10:13.112 align:start size:88% position:13%
and the women of Fox
who had all these issues

00:10:13.112 --> 00:10:14.880
and for any victim.

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:16.682
Anyone is truly a victim.

00:10:16.682 --> 00:10:19.885
Remember as a prosecutor,
I had to decide is it real?

00:10:19.885 --> 00:10:21.053
Is it credible?

00:10:21.053 --> 00:10:22.288
Is it believable?

00:10:22.288 --> 00:10:24.290
Does it make sense?

00:10:24.290 --> 00:10:26.092
You know, my heart
goes out to them

00:10:26.092 --> 00:10:29.261
and my passion went out to
them along with my energy.

00:10:30.696 --> 00:10:33.466
But in the movie that they
made and I never saw it,

00:10:33.466 --> 00:10:37.269
they portrayed me as someone
who was, as I understand it,

00:10:37.269 --> 00:10:42.141
apologetic for you
know, the offenders.

00:10:42.141 --> 00:10:45.311 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, yeah.
- And it made me crazy, Dave.

00:10:45.311 --> 00:10:46.712
It made me nuts.

00:10:46.712 --> 00:10:51.450
I said to myself for
all those #MeToo women,

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:56.389 align:start size:97% position:3%
where the hell were you in
the late 70s and the early 80s

00:10:56.389 --> 00:10:58.658
when I was fighting for
children to testify?

00:10:58.658 --> 00:11:02.795
Where the hell were you when I
was trying to make it illegal

00:11:02.795 --> 00:11:04.797
to put women on a
lie detector test

00:11:04.797 --> 00:11:06.332
to claim that they were raped?

00:11:06.332 --> 00:11:08.200 align:start size:84% position:16%
Where were you
when I was fighting

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:10.503 align:start size:88% position:13%
to make sure that women
couldn't be judged

00:11:10.503 --> 00:11:12.004
because they wore a short skirt

00:11:12.004 --> 00:11:14.073 align:start size:88% position:13%
which then allows a
defense attorney to say,

00:11:14.073 --> 00:11:15.541
"You were askin' for it"?

00:11:15.541 --> 00:11:17.009
You wear a Rolex.

00:11:17.009 --> 00:11:21.313 align:start size:94% position:6%
If you're burglarized
Dave or someone robs you,

00:11:21.313 --> 00:11:23.916
they're not gonna say,
"Hey, you wore that Rolex

00:11:23.916 --> 00:11:25.584
'cause you want
it to be robbed."

00:11:25.584 --> 00:11:27.053
But a woman wears a short skirt,

00:11:27.053 --> 00:11:29.488
"She wore that skirt 'cause
she wanted to be raped."

00:11:29.488 --> 00:11:32.558
You know, it makes me crazy

00:11:32.558 --> 00:11:35.294
because I've spent
my life fighting

00:11:35.294 --> 00:11:38.998
for the silent victims
of crime, for women,

00:11:38.998 --> 00:11:40.833
for the elderly, for children.

00:11:40.833 --> 00:11:43.602 align:start size:88% position:13%
People have no idea
what happens to children

00:11:43.602 --> 00:11:45.337
in our system today.

00:11:45.337 --> 00:11:49.408 align:start position:0%
You know, I started getting
reports of suspected child abuse

00:11:49.408 --> 00:11:52.044
in the late 70s and
they said to me,

00:11:52.044 --> 00:11:54.046
"You're not entitled to those."

00:11:54.046 --> 00:11:55.948
I said, "Yes, I am."

00:11:55.948 --> 00:11:58.484 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean there's a certain
benefit to naivete.

00:11:58.484 --> 00:12:01.921 align:start size:97% position:3%
In my moral core said,
"Bologna, of course you are."

00:12:01.921 --> 00:12:04.356
And they said, "No,
these are privileged.

00:12:04.356 --> 00:12:06.659
They stay with social services."

00:12:06.659 --> 00:12:08.294
So I had an all-out war

00:12:08.294 --> 00:12:10.930 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I was gonna sue the
Department of Social Services.

00:12:10.930 --> 00:12:14.166 align:start size:97% position:3%
I went to the DA and he says,
"Oh, let little Jeanine.

00:12:14.166 --> 00:12:16.669
Let her go do her
social things."

00:12:16.669 --> 00:12:18.370 align:start size:81% position:19%
And it turned out,
I said to them,

00:12:18.370 --> 00:12:22.274
"Look, if a social worker
goes and finds that a child

00:12:22.274 --> 00:12:27.146 align:start size:84% position:16%
is maybe being abused
but returns the
child to the parents,

00:12:27.146 --> 00:12:29.815
that record is then expunged.

00:12:29.815 --> 00:12:31.250 align:start size:84% position:16%
And maybe if
they're called again

00:12:31.250 --> 00:12:34.587 align:start size:97% position:3%
and they don't find anything,
that record is expunged.

00:12:34.587 --> 00:12:37.990 align:start size:84% position:16%
But the third time
the kid is dead, okay?

00:12:37.990 --> 00:12:40.926
I want the first record
and the second record

00:12:40.926 --> 00:12:42.728
and don't you dare tell me

00:12:42.728 --> 00:12:46.165
that I can't get a history
of abuse in a criminal case

00:12:46.165 --> 00:12:48.667 align:start size:88% position:13%
when I've got the
homicide of that child."

00:12:48.667 --> 00:12:50.703
And I did child abuse homicides,

00:12:50.703 --> 00:12:52.238
that was one of my expertise.

00:12:52.238 --> 00:12:54.106 align:start size:84% position:16%
Whether it's shaken
baby syndrome,

00:12:54.106 --> 00:12:57.576 align:start size:97% position:3%
immersion in scalding water,
being beaten to death.

00:12:57.576 --> 00:12:59.979
I mean I can tell you stories
that'd make your hair stand.

00:12:59.979 --> 00:13:02.047 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I don't talk
about it often, Dave.

00:13:02.047 --> 00:13:05.417
And I don't know, you just
kinda hit a thing in my head.

00:13:05.417 --> 00:13:07.820
Very rarely do people
even know what I did

00:13:07.820 --> 00:13:10.389
other than, "Oh she was the
DA, whatever that means."

00:13:10.389 --> 00:13:11.924
But no, I have a passion

00:13:13.025 --> 00:13:16.595 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I have an empathy
for the victim.

00:13:16.595 --> 00:13:19.465
The victim was everything to me.

00:13:19.465 --> 00:13:21.600
Why do we name the system, Dave?

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:22.601
Think about this.

00:13:22.601 --> 00:13:25.037
The criminal justice system.

00:13:25.037 --> 00:13:27.506 align:start size:84% position:16%
It should be the
victim justice system.

00:13:27.506 --> 00:13:30.643 line:0%
The victim never wanted
to be a part of this.

00:13:30.643 --> 00:13:32.745 line:0%
But all of a sudden
like a thunderbolt,

00:13:32.745 --> 00:13:34.547 line:0%
their lives are changed

00:13:34.547 --> 00:13:38.450 align:start size:97% position:3%
and the ripple effect to their
family and the community,

00:13:38.450 --> 00:13:41.654 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's enormous and you know,
what we're seeing today

00:13:41.654 --> 00:13:44.924
is just an example of
all that craziness.

00:13:44.924 --> 00:13:49.028
- So when you hear people
talk about systemic racism

00:13:49.028 --> 00:13:52.198 align:start size:88% position:13%
that is in our criminal
justice system.

00:13:52.198 --> 00:13:55.534
Well I guess first, do
you think that exists?

00:13:55.534 --> 00:14:00.206
And if it does exist, can
you gimme an example of it

00:14:00.206 --> 00:14:02.241
or a time that you
came up against it?

00:14:04.476 --> 00:14:07.613 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I absolutely do not believe
there's systemic racism.

00:14:07.613 --> 00:14:11.283 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think that the laws are
in place to protect everyone

00:14:11.283 --> 00:14:16.021
and I watched those laws
evolve over the years.

00:14:16.021 --> 00:14:17.723
But in terms of what I've done,

00:14:17.723 --> 00:14:20.125
lemme be real clear about this.

00:14:20.125 --> 00:14:21.527
Are there bad cops?

00:14:21.527 --> 00:14:23.395
Yes, absolutely.

00:14:23.395 --> 00:14:25.464
Have I prosecuted bad cops?

00:14:25.464 --> 00:14:27.199
Yes, absolutely.

00:14:27.199 --> 00:14:28.467
Did I like it?

00:14:28.467 --> 00:14:30.336 align:start size:84% position:16%
I didn't really like
it or dislike it.

00:14:30.336 --> 00:14:33.005
It was the only
way to handle it.

00:14:33.005 --> 00:14:38.010 align:start size:97% position:3%
I prosecuted an off-duty
New York City police officer

00:14:38.878 --> 00:14:40.212
for shooting an African American

00:14:40.212 --> 00:14:43.115 align:start size:84% position:16%
over a parking space
in Westchester.

00:14:43.115 --> 00:14:45.885
Dave, to this day,

00:14:47.019 --> 00:14:49.321
that guy who was the cop.

00:14:49.321 --> 00:14:50.189
I convicted him.

00:14:50.189 --> 00:14:51.023
He was convicted.

00:14:51.023 --> 00:14:52.491
My office convicted him.

00:14:52.491 --> 00:14:54.927 align:start size:97% position:3%
He was convicted and he
went to prison for 25 years.

00:14:54.927 --> 00:14:59.098
To this day, these guys are
after me for doing that.

00:14:59.098 --> 00:15:02.468
Now I had no idea.

00:15:02.468 --> 00:15:04.136
But Al Sharpton who I knew

00:15:04.136 --> 00:15:07.373
'cause I ran for office
you know, so many times.

00:15:07.373 --> 00:15:09.041
Al Sharpton wanted
to make a visit.

00:15:09.041 --> 00:15:11.744 align:start size:97% position:3%
He called my secretary, at the
time there were secretaries

00:15:11.744 --> 00:15:14.680 align:start size:97% position:3%
and she said, "Boss, Al
Sharpton wants to visit you

00:15:14.680 --> 00:15:15.648
with a few reverends."

00:15:15.648 --> 00:15:17.483
I said, "Why not?"

00:15:17.483 --> 00:15:19.485
You know, that's fine.

00:15:19.485 --> 00:15:21.887 align:start size:94% position:6%
So they came in one
morning after the verdict

00:15:21.887 --> 00:15:24.757 align:start size:97% position:3%
about three or four days
and they brought in pastries

00:15:24.757 --> 00:15:26.392
and we made coffee for them.

00:15:26.392 --> 00:15:28.761
And you know, I sat and I
kinda waited for a second.

00:15:28.761 --> 00:15:30.429
And they said, "We
wanna thank you."

00:15:30.429 --> 00:15:32.264
And I said, "For what?"

00:15:32.264 --> 00:15:36.101
And they said, "Never in
the history of New York

00:15:36.101 --> 00:15:40.239 align:start size:97% position:3%
has a white cop been convicted
of killing a black man."

00:15:41.407 --> 00:15:44.710
And I didn't realize
that it was historic.

00:15:44.710 --> 00:15:45.544
Am I proud of it?

00:15:45.544 --> 00:15:47.246
No, I'm not proud of it.

00:15:47.246 --> 00:15:48.480
I'm not ashamed of it.

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:50.382 align:start size:84% position:16%
It was a horrible
thing that happened,

00:15:50.382 --> 00:15:52.217 align:start size:81% position:19%
but it was the
right thing to do.

00:15:52.217 --> 00:15:54.453
But to this day when the NYPD

00:15:54.453 --> 00:15:57.957
gave me an award two years
ago, that guy's friends,

00:15:57.957 --> 00:15:59.892 align:start size:88% position:13%
the white cop's friends
from New York City,

00:15:59.892 --> 00:16:00.826
they're still after me.

00:16:00.826 --> 00:16:03.062
I have a radio show WABC.

00:16:03.062 --> 00:16:05.364 align:start position:0%
They call me up to say, "You're
this and that," you know?

00:16:05.364 --> 00:16:06.565
And then I just go like that.

00:16:06.565 --> 00:16:08.167
They get rid of the call.

00:16:08.167 --> 00:16:13.172
But I've come across a lot
of cases that were ugly.

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:16.508
But if your moral
core is strong,

00:16:16.508 --> 00:16:18.978
if you know who you are,

00:16:18.978 --> 00:16:22.982
if you're not a weak
link, then it's easy.

00:16:22.982 --> 00:16:24.416
You do what you have to do.

00:16:25.751 --> 00:16:27.386 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So actually I wasn't
gonna ask you this,

00:16:27.386 --> 00:16:29.254 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I think it's sorta
the perfect segue

00:16:29.254 --> 00:16:30.189
before we get to the book.

00:16:30.189 --> 00:16:32.358
So speakin' of moral core

00:16:32.358 --> 00:16:34.560
in doin' what you think
is right and all that,

00:16:34.560 --> 00:16:35.694
you're a New Yorker.

00:16:35.694 --> 00:16:37.696 align:start size:88% position:13%
As you know, I'm
born-and-bred New Yorker

00:16:37.696 --> 00:16:40.065
until I moved out to
Cali eight years ago,

00:16:40.065 --> 00:16:42.401 align:start size:88% position:13%
so I've only lived in
New York and California.

00:16:43.836 --> 00:16:46.138 align:start size:84% position:16%
What is it do you
think about New York?

00:16:46.138 --> 00:16:48.273
Like you strike me
as such a New Yorker,

00:16:48.273 --> 00:16:51.210
like in the best sense of
what New York is, you know?

00:16:51.210 --> 00:16:53.579
People think if you're from New
York and Cali, you're crazy.

00:16:53.579 --> 00:16:58.083
But I mean New York in just
sort of the ethnic makeup

00:16:58.083 --> 00:17:01.387
of New York and the no BS and
call it as it is and all that.

00:17:01.387 --> 00:17:02.321
Can you just tell me
a little bit about

00:17:02.321 --> 00:17:04.189
just like your family makeup

00:17:04.189 --> 00:17:05.024
and where some of
that came from?

00:17:05.024 --> 00:17:05.924
- Okay, sure.

00:17:05.924 --> 00:17:07.960
I'd be happy to.

00:17:07.960 --> 00:17:09.895
I'm of Lebanese descent.

00:17:09.895 --> 00:17:11.096 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't know if
you even knew that.

00:17:11.096 --> 00:17:14.133 align:start size:88% position:13%
Everyone assumes because
I married an Italian

00:17:14.133 --> 00:17:15.334 align:start size:88% position:13%
and my name is Jeanine.
- Italian.

00:17:15.334 --> 00:17:17.069 align:start size:84% position:16%
Yeah, I thought
you were a purebred

00:17:17.069 --> 00:17:19.204
off-the-boat from Sicily.

00:17:19.204 --> 00:17:20.406
- No, no.

00:17:20.406 --> 00:17:23.776
I'm Lebanese Christian,
both my mom and dad.

00:17:23.776 --> 00:17:25.844
My dad fought in World War II.

00:17:25.844 --> 00:17:27.279
He was not first generation.

00:17:27.279 --> 00:17:29.248 align:start size:84% position:16%
My grandfather fought
in World War II,

00:17:29.248 --> 00:17:33.018
but I'm 100% Lebanese Christian.

00:17:34.720 --> 00:17:36.422
We kept the subculture alive.

00:17:36.422 --> 00:17:37.589
I cook the food.

00:17:37.589 --> 00:17:39.258 align:start size:88% position:13%
I cook hummus, tabbouleh
and baba ganoush;

00:17:39.258 --> 00:17:40.893
You know, the grape leaves.

00:17:40.893 --> 00:17:42.594
I do all that stuff.

00:17:42.594 --> 00:17:44.129
But I'm gonna surprise you.

00:17:44.129 --> 00:17:46.899 align:start size:88% position:13%
I grew up in a small
town in upstate New York

00:17:46.899 --> 00:17:50.269 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I worked in a
dairy as a young kid.

00:17:52.604 --> 00:17:55.474
You know, people say to me,
"Are you from the Bronx?

00:17:55.474 --> 00:17:58.110
Are you from (drowned
out by poor audio)?"

00:17:58.110 --> 00:17:59.845
No, actually I'm from Elmira.

00:17:59.845 --> 00:18:02.147
And they're like,
"Where is that?"

00:18:02.147 --> 00:18:05.451 align:start size:84% position:16%
And in fact Dave for
the longest time,

00:18:05.451 --> 00:18:08.987
I had potbelly pigs in my
yard here in Westchester.

00:18:08.987 --> 00:18:11.690 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know, the pigs.
(imitates pig snorting)

00:18:11.690 --> 00:18:13.325
Those pigs.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:18:13.325 --> 00:18:16.028 align:start size:84% position:16%
- And one lived I
think it was 16 years.

00:18:16.028 --> 00:18:18.263 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean I just
liked farm animals.

00:18:19.698 --> 00:18:23.836
You know, upstate New York
is more like middle America,

00:18:23.836 --> 00:18:24.870
you know?

00:18:24.870 --> 00:18:26.905
I learned how to shoot a gun.

00:18:28.006 --> 00:18:29.441
Church was important.

00:18:29.441 --> 00:18:30.709
Education was important.

00:18:30.709 --> 00:18:33.345
I'm the first one in my
family to go to college.

00:18:34.780 --> 00:18:39.118
And you know, it was a very
simple, very simple life.

00:18:39.985 --> 00:18:41.620
Nothing complicated about it.

00:18:41.620 --> 00:18:46.024
And my mom passed last year in
the room right above us here.

00:18:46.024 --> 00:18:48.026
I had her in hospice.

00:18:48.026 --> 00:18:49.895
You know, she was everything.

00:18:49.895 --> 00:18:54.199 align:start position:0%
She gave me you know, the
moral compass to both empathize

00:18:55.367 --> 00:18:58.537
and she gave me that
heart for victims.

00:18:58.537 --> 00:19:01.607 align:start position:0%
You know, they just weren't
people that you prosecuted for.

00:19:01.607 --> 00:19:02.808
They were your family.

00:19:04.710 --> 00:19:09.214
And yet the tough side of me
came from being a prosecutor.

00:19:09.214 --> 00:19:10.749
You know, I was the first woman

00:19:10.749 --> 00:19:12.451
that went out on
a homicide review.

00:19:12.451 --> 00:19:15.220 align:start size:88% position:13%
And you know, I
remember the first time

00:19:15.220 --> 00:19:18.390
I went out on a homicide call.

00:19:18.390 --> 00:19:19.825
I went out.

00:19:19.825 --> 00:19:21.994 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, like the first
thing I said you know, laugh.

00:19:21.994 --> 00:19:24.596 align:start size:97% position:3%
The call comes in of course
at 3:00 in the morning.

00:19:24.596 --> 00:19:26.999
Homicides only happen
at 3:00 in the morning.

00:19:26.999 --> 00:19:31.103 align:start size:97% position:3%
So after they have an
argument with my then-husband

00:19:31.103 --> 00:19:34.139
that he was gonna get his
wife and they kept saying,

00:19:34.139 --> 00:19:37.042
"No, we want the assistant
DA," 'cause I was the first.

00:19:38.777 --> 00:19:42.181
(chuckles) So yeah,
I say to my husband

00:19:42.181 --> 00:19:44.149
after they tell me the location.

00:19:44.149 --> 00:19:46.451
I say, "What do you
wear to a homicide?"

00:19:47.853 --> 00:19:50.889 align:start size:97% position:3%
(laughs) So I'm like, "Oh god,
what a dumb thing to say."

00:19:50.889 --> 00:19:52.824 align:start size:97% position:3%
He said, "It doesn't matter
what you wear, Jeanine.

00:19:52.824 --> 00:19:54.993
Just take the penal
law along with you."

00:19:54.993 --> 00:19:57.729 align:start position:0%
So when I got there, they didn't
wanna let me on the scene.

00:19:57.729 --> 00:19:58.664
And I said, "No, no.

00:19:58.664 --> 00:19:59.731
I'm really an assistant DA."

00:19:59.731 --> 00:20:02.467
I was like 25 years old.

00:20:02.467 --> 00:20:05.470
So yeah, it's been an
interesting career.

00:20:07.139 --> 00:20:09.908
I'm very, very lucky
I had a simple life

00:20:09.908 --> 00:20:12.311
to deal with this
complicated world.

00:20:12.311 --> 00:20:14.780 align:start size:94% position:6%
- I'm pretty sure the
title of your next book is

00:20:14.780 --> 00:20:16.748
"What Do Ya Wear to a Homicide?"

00:20:20.385 --> 00:20:21.887
I think we got that right there.

00:20:21.887 --> 00:20:23.922 align:start size:94% position:6%
Oh and by the way, for the
record I do know Elmira

00:20:23.922 --> 00:20:25.591
because I went to
SUNY, Binghamton.

00:20:25.591 --> 00:20:28.026 align:start size:94% position:6%
- No.
- So I know a little bit.

00:20:28.026 --> 00:20:31.496
I know a little bit about
the southern tier, yeah.

00:20:31.496 --> 00:20:32.431
- Oh my gosh, WENY?

00:20:33.932 --> 00:20:34.800
- Yeah.

00:20:34.800 --> 00:20:36.535
- Do you remember WENY?

00:20:36.535 --> 00:20:37.336
- Of course I do.

00:20:38.203 --> 00:20:39.004
- Did you ski?

00:20:40.672 --> 00:20:44.176
- Not well, I could fall down
a mountain pretty decently.

00:20:44.176 --> 00:20:45.010
- Yeah?

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:46.511
Oh my gosh.

00:20:46.511 --> 00:20:48.680 align:start size:94% position:6%
Yeah. My grandfather lived
in Binghamton anyway.

00:20:48.680 --> 00:20:49.715
- Yeah, all right.

00:20:49.715 --> 00:20:52.584
So let's shift a
little bit here.

00:20:52.584 --> 00:20:55.087
I know that this
interview right now

00:20:55.087 --> 00:20:57.856
is obviously your biggest
interview of the week.

